1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,000
The first time you stream a response from Cloud,

2
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:04,000
the output shows up in pieces,

3
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:07,000
and it's easy to wonder what's actually happening on the wire.

4
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:12,000
This lesson covers how streaming works over a plain HTTPS connection,

5
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:14,000
the fixed order of events you get back,

6
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:17,000
and how you rebuild them into one response.

7
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:20,000
Let's dive in. Let's lock down

8
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:23,000
three fundamentals because the rest of the lesson builds on them.

9
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:25,000
First, the connection.

10
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:30,000
You reach the Cloud API over a normal encrypted HTTP request.

11
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:33,000
There's no exotic protocol, no special socket.

12
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:37,000
It's the same kind of request your browser makes to load a page.

13
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:39,000
Second, streaming mode.

14
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:41,000
By default, the server builds

15
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:44,000
your whole response and hands it back in one piece.

16
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:46,000
When you set stream to true,

17
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:50,000
you're asking it to send the answer incrementally as it's produced.

18
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:53,000
Third, the format those pieces travel in.

19
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:56,000
Cloud uses server sent events.

20
00:00:56,000 --> 00:00:59,000
It's a simple standard where the server pushes

21
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:02,000
named events down the open connection,

22
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:04,000
one direction only from server to client.

23
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:08,000
Connection, streaming flag, server sent events.

24
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:10,000
Those three frame everything.

25
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:13,000
What actually happens when you turn streaming on?

26
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:16,000
Almost nothing changes about the call itself.

27
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:18,000
You hit the same endpoint with

28
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:21,000
the same request body and you add the streaming flag.

29
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:23,000
What changes is the response.

30
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:25,000
Instead of closing quickly with a finished message,

31
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:28,000
the server keeps one HTTP response

32
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:30,000
open and writes to it over time.

33
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:36,000
Each piece of output arrives as a labeled event with a small JSON payload.

34
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:40,000
Your client reads those events off the connection as they land,

35
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:44,000
and the connection stays alive right up until the final event ends it.

36
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:46,000
The mental model is simple.

37
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:49,000
You're not polling and you're not opening a second channel.

38
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:54,000
You're reading one response as the server writes it, piece by piece.

39
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:57,000
Let's compare the two ways a response can come back.

40
00:01:57,000 --> 00:01:59,000
Because the trade-off matters,

41
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:00,000
the default is buffered.

42
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:03,000
The server generates the entire message,

43
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:05,000
then returns it in one response.

44
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:07,000
It's dead simple to handle.

45
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:08,000
You call, you wait,

46
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:10,000
you get one complete object.

47
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:12,000
The cost is that waiting.

48
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:15,000
Nothing appears until the whole thing is finished.

49
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:16,000
Streaming flips that.

50
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:18,000
With the flag on,

51
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:20,000
tokens reach you as they're generated.

52
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:21,000
Two things get better.

53
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:23,000
First, perceived speed.

54
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:28,000
Your user sees text appear right away instead of watching a spinner.

55
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:32,000
Second, and this is the practical one for builders, long responses.

56
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:34,000
A big generation can take long enough that

57
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:37,000
a single blocking call would hit a timeout.

58
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:39,000
Streaming keeps data flowing,

59
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:42,000
so the connection never sits idle long enough to drop.

60
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:46,000
Here's something that makes streaming much easier to work with.

61
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:50,000
The events don't show up in some unpredictable jumble.

62
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:54,000
They follow one guaranteed order every single time.

63
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:57,000
It opens with a message, start event.

64
00:02:57,000 --> 00:02:59,000
That event carries a message object,

65
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:01,000
but the content is empty.

66
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:03,000
So don't go looking for text in it.

67
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:06,000
It's just the signal that the response has begun.

68
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:09,000
After that come the content block events.

69
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:11,000
This is where the real payload lives,

70
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:14,000
the actual text tokens.

71
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:15,000
And for tool calls,

72
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:18,000
the tool input arriving in fragments.

73
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:19,000
Then near the end,

74
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:23,000
you get a message stop event that marks the response complete.

75
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:25,000
Because that order is fixed,

76
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:28,000
your code always knows what to expect next.

77
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:30,000
You're never guessing whether more is coming.

78
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:33,000
The structure tells you.

79
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:36,000
Let me point out three events that frame every stream

80
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:38,000
because they anchor the whole sequence.

81
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:40,000
The first is message start.

82
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:45,000
It opens the stream and hands you a message with empty content. Remember that.

83
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:48,000
The very first event is never the text itself.

84
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:50,000
It's just the opening bracket.

85
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:53,000
In the middle sit the content block delta events.

86
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:55,000
These are the workhorses.

87
00:03:55,000 --> 00:03:57,000
Each one carries a small piece of output,

88
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:01,000
a chunk of text, or a slice of tool input.

89
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:03,000
And there are usually many of them.

90
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:05,000
Then comes message stop.

91
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:06,000
That's the closing bracket.

92
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:08,000
The moment you receive it,

93
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:11,000
the response is finished and the connection can close.

94
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:14,000
Start, deltas, stop.

95
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:15,000
Handle those three correctly

96
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:19,000
and you've got the backbone of a streaming reader.

97
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:21,000
Now let's talk about putting the pieces back together

98
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:24,000
because a streamed response arrives in fragments

99
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:26,000
and your code reassembles it.

100
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:28,000
The pattern is straightforward.

101
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:30,000
When the message start event lands,

102
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:32,000
you set up an empty buffer.

103
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:34,000
Then as each delta event comes in,

104
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:38,000
you append its chunk of text onto that buffer.

105
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:40,000
Piece by piece, the full message builds up.

106
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:43,000
When a content block's stop event arrives,

107
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:44,000
that block is done.

108
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:47,000
And when the final message stop event fires,

109
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:50,000
your buffer holds the complete response,

110
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:53,000
exactly what a single buffered call would have returned.

111
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:56,000
The good news is that the official SDKs

112
00:04:56,000 --> 00:05:00,000
do this accumulation for you and hand back finished text.

113
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:03,000
But it helps to know what's happening underneath,

114
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:04,000
especially when you're debugging.

115
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:08,000
Let's get more precise about the delta events

116
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:10,000
because there's more than one kind.

117
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:13,000
The most common is the content block delta.

118
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:17,000
Each one adds a chunk of output to a specific content block

119
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:19,000
and most of your streamed text

120
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:22,000
arrives as a long series of them.

121
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:24,000
Then there's the message delta.

122
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:27,000
These show up later and carry top-level information

123
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:29,000
about the response as a whole.

124
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:32,000
Things like the stop reason and the token usage counts.

125
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:37,000
And third, for tool calls, there's the input JSON delta.

126
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:39,000
This one's special.

127
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:42,000
It carries partial JSON, fragments of a tool's arguments

128
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:45,000
that build up gradually across several events.

129
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:48,000
Content block delta for output.

130
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:50,000
Message delta for the wrap-up.

131
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:54,000
Input JSON delta for tool arguments.

132
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:56,000
Same idea, three flavors.

133
00:05:57,000 --> 00:06:00,000
That last one, the partial JSON, trips people up.

134
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:02,000
So let's slow down on it.

135
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:04,000
When Claude decides to call a tool,

136
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:08,000
it doesn't hand you the arguments in one clean object.

137
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:10,000
They stream in as partial JSON,

138
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:13,000
small fragments spread across several delta events.

139
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:15,000
Here's the catch.

140
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:18,000
A half-built fragment isn't valid JSON on its own.

141
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:21,000
If you parse it midstream, it'll fail

142
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:23,000
because the object isn't finished.

143
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:25,000
So the rule is simple.

144
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:28,000
Accumulate the fragments as they arrive,

145
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:31,000
but wait to parse until the content block's stop event lands.

146
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:33,000
At that point, and only then,

147
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:37,000
the tool input is guaranteed to be a complete, valid object.

148
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:39,000
So don't parse early.

149
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:42,000
Collect everything, wait for the stop signal,

150
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:43,000
then parse once.

151
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:48,000
That habit saves a lot of confusing errors. Let's recap.

152
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:51,000
Streaming is just server-sent events

153
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:54,000
flowing over one ordinary HTTPS response.

154
00:06:54,000 --> 00:06:56,000
The events come in a fixed order,

155
00:06:56,000 --> 00:06:59,000
and you accumulate deltas into a buffer

156
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:01,000
to rebuild the message.

157
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:02,000
One thing to watch.

158
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:05,000
Tool arguments arrive as partial JSON,

159
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:08,000
so parse them only at the block's close.

160
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:12,000
That covers streaming over HTTP with server-sent events.

161
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:14,000
I'll see you in the next lesson.

